# Everything in the frontend works on mobile

Any screen, section, card, table, dialog or control you add or change in v0 **must work on a
phone (375px wide)**, not only on desktop. A change that only looks right on desktop is not done.

## Do

- **Grids start at one column** and add columns at breakpoints: `grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4`.
  Never a bare `grid-cols-3` / `grid-cols-4` without a `grid-cols-1` (or `grid-cols-2` for tiny tiles) base.
- **Card grids flow by available width, not by a fixed column count.** Cards (KPI cards, tiles) use
  `grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,20rem),1fr))]` (pick the min width the card needs), so a
  card drops to the next row when it no longer fits instead of squeezing its text — this matters at
  every width (1200px with the sidebar open too), not only on phones. A fixed `lg:grid-cols-4` is only
  OK when every card is guaranteed to fit at that breakpoint.
- **Flex rows that hold several items wrap or stack** on small screens: `flex-wrap`, or `flex-col sm:flex-row`.
- **Flex children with text get `min-w-0`** (and `truncate` or wrapping), so long text shrinks instead of
  pushing the row past the screen.
- **Fixed widths only with a mobile fallback**: `w-full sm:w-[240px]`, never a bare `w-[480px]` on a container.
- **Wide content scrolls inside its own box**, not the page: tables go in `DataTable` / `overflow-x-auto`
  containers; charts use responsive containers.
- **Dialogs and sheets fit the screen**: `max-w-[calc(100%-2rem)]` / full width on mobile.
- **Type scales down on mobile.** Page titles, section headings, big KPI numbers and icons are
  smaller below `sm`: e.g. `text-2xl sm:text-3xl` for a page title, `text-lg sm:text-xl` for a section
  heading, `text-lg sm:text-[22px]` for a KPI value, `h-10 w-10 sm:h-14 sm:w-14` for a heading icon.
  Desktop sizes never apply unchanged to a 375px screen.
- **Tabs and toolbars** with many items use a grid or wrap that fits 375px (e.g. `grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4`).

## Sticky headers: no seam, no jitter

Anything `position: sticky` over content that scrolls under it (table headers, group headers in a
sheet, toolbars) shows a **1px line of the rows scrolling through above it** and **jitters while
scrolling** on phones: the scroll box lands on a fractional pixel and the browser snaps its clip
edge and the sticky element to different pixels.

- **Tables: use the shared `DataTable`** (`components/shared/data-table`) when it fits.
- A table built by hand inside a sheet or box **does not use a sticky header**. The header goes
  **outside the vertical scroller**: an outer box with `overflow-x-auto` (one horizontal scroll for
  both), the header table on top, and the rows in their own `overflow-y-auto` box below; both tables
  share the same `colgroup` and both parts get `[scrollbar-gutter:stable]` so the columns line up.
  A 1px `box-shadow` band was tried and **did not fix it** in Chrome's phone view (2026-10-13).
  Reference: `components/roles/role-services-sheet.tsx`.
- **No fixed (pinned) columns on a phone**, and the first column's text wraps instead of «…»: detail in
  `data-table.mdc` → «Columnas pinned (sticky)» (Ignacio, 2026-10-27, Production vs Goal).
- Don't add a page margin a layout already gives: `MainLayout` pads the page (`p-4 md:p-6`), so a
  page's root `div` adds none — otherwise phones lose 16px per side for nothing.

## Check before delivering

1. Open the screen in the Browser pane and switch to mobile: `resize_window` with `preset: "mobile"` (375×812).
2. Confirm there is **no horizontal page scroll** (`document.documentElement.scrollWidth <= window.innerWidth`)
   and that nothing is cut at the right edge.
3. If the screen has a fixed header over scrolling rows, **scroll the content up and down at mobile width**
   and zoom on the header's top edge: no line of rows above it, no jumping. Ignacio checks with Chrome's
   phone view (DevTools device mode) on his Mac; a plain 390px window does not show the seam, so a
   check there is not proof — say so.
4. Take a mobile screenshot as proof, then reset with `preset: "desktop"`.

If you cannot open the screen (for example it needs a login you can't enter), say so explicitly — do not
report the change as verified on mobile.

## Do NOT

- Ship a layout that was only looked at on desktop.
- Let a single wide element (long title, fixed-width input, table, chart) widen the whole page.
- Hide content on mobile to "fix" overflow unless the user asked for it.

## Why

On 2026-09-18 Ignacio found the Dashboard cut off at the right on a phone right after a KPI card change
was delivered without a mobile check. On 2026-10-13 the Roles > Services sheet (a hand-built table)
shipped with the header seam and jitter on phones, and the Roles pages with a doubled side margin.
